  1+  8389              ; MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Cronomantic
  2+  8389              ;
  3+  8389              ; The screen on an MSX1, which is not in the processor's memory at all.
  4+  8389              ;
  5+  8389              ; It lives in the video chip's own sixteen kilobytes, behind two ports: one to
  6+  8389              ; say which address, one to pass the bytes.  Reading it back costs two writes
  7+  8389              ; and a read, where a Spectrum costs seven clock cycles, and our fill reads the
  8+  8389              ; screen constantly -- it walks left and right asking what is set.
  9+  8389              ;
 10+  8389              ; So the picture is drawn into a copy in our own memory and sent across when it
 11+  8389              ; is finished.  Everything above that is then the Spectrum's, unchanged: screen
 12+  8389              ; 2 is one bit per pixel and a fill stops where a pixel is set, exactly as
 13+  8389              ; there.  What is not the Spectrum's is the colour, which belongs to eight
 14+  8389              ; pixels of one line rather than to a cell of eight by eight.
 15+  8389              ;
 16+  8389              ; The copy is kept **row by row**, thirty two bytes to a line, and not in the
 17+  8389              ; shape the chip wants -- which is the eight lines of a cell together, at
 18+  8389              ;
 19+  8389              ;       (y & $F8) * 32 + (x & $F8) + (y & 7)
 20+  8389              ;
 21+  8389              ; That was tried the other way round first, and it cost: in the chip's shape
 22+  8389              ; the byte next to this one along a row is eight further on, so the fill's
 23+  8389              ; search for the ends of a run steps with three instructions where the
 24+  8389              ; Spectrum's steps with one, twice for every cell of every row.  Measured on a
 25+  8389              ; screenful, that search took 0.32s against the Spectrum's 0.18s while writing
 26+  8389              ; the pixels and the colours cost the same on both.
 27+  8389              ;
 28+  8389              ; Laid out row by row, all of that is the Spectrum's code unchanged, and the
 29+  8389              ; shuffling into the chip's shape happens once, in the sending, which walks
 30+  8389              ; the whole copy anyway: eight bytes of a cell are eight steps of thirty two.
 31+  8389              ;
 32+  8389              ; The text goes straight to the chip: printing never reads what is there.
 33+  8389
 34+  8389              VDP_DATA        equ $98                 ; the bytes
 35+  8389              VDP_ADDR        equ $99                 ; where they go, and the registers
 36+  8389
 37+  8389              VRAM_PATTERNS   equ $0000               ; the three tables, in the chip
 38+  8389              VRAM_NAMES      equ $1800
 39+  8389              VRAM_COLOURS    equ $2000
 40+  8389              VRAM_COLOUR_ROW equ (VRAM_COLOURS - VRAM_PATTERNS) >> 8         ; rows apart
 41+  8389              VRAM_SHOWN      equ VRAM_COLOURS + $1800        ; patterns, names and colours:
 42+  8389                                                              ; what a dump of this screen is
 43+  8389              ROW_BYTES       equ 256                 ; one character row of either table
 44+  8389
 45+  8389              SCREEN_COLS     equ 32
 46+  8389              TEXT_TOP        equ 16                  ; the first row the text may use
 47+  8389              TEXT_ROWS       equ 24 - TEXT_TOP
 48+  8389              PICTURE_ROWS    equ TEXT_TOP * 8        ; the picture, in pixel lines
 49+  8389              PICTURE_BYTES   equ TEXT_TOP * ROW_BYTES
 50+  8389
 51+  8389              ; And the copy we draw into: four kilobytes of patterns and four of colours,
 52+  8389              ; row by row, on a four kilobyte boundary so that the eight rows of a page are
 53+  8389              ; the high byte of an address and nothing else.
 54+  8389              SHADOW          equ $C000
 55+  8389              SHADOW_COLOURS  equ SHADOW + $1000
 56+  8389
 57+  8389              ; Colours, as this machine numbers them.  A picture asks in the Spectrum's
 58+  8389              ; sixteen and gets the nearest of the fifteen here; the table that does the
 59+  8389              ; matching is in draw.asm and comes from the reference renderer, so that both
 60+  8389              ; ends of the comparison agree.
 61+  8389              MSX_BLACK       equ 1
 62+  8389              MSX_WHITE       equ 14
 63+  8389              TEXT_COLOUR     equ (MSX_WHITE << 4) | MSX_BLACK
 64+  8389
 65+  8389              ; The eight registers screen 2 wants.
 66+  8389 02           screen_setup:   db      $02             ; graphics two
 67+  838A C0                           db      $C0             ; sixteen K, display on, no interrupt
 68+  838B 06                           db      VRAM_NAMES / $400
 69+  838C FF                           db      $FF             ; the colour table, and all of it
 70+  838D 03                           db      $03             ; the patterns
 71+  838E 36                           db      $36             ; sprites, which are never used
 72+  838F 07                           db      $07
 73+  8390 01                           db      MSX_BLACK       ; the border
 74+  8391
 75+  8391              ; The eight registers, and nothing else: the loader wants this on its own, to
 76+  8391              ; put a loading screen up before there is a database to take a font out of.
 77+  8391              ; Corrupts: AF, C, HL
 78+  8391              vdp_setup:
 79+  8391 21 89 83                     ld      hl, screen_setup
